panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].panel_count: Field required; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].panels: Field required; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].categories_per_panel: Extra inputs are not permitted; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].n_panels: Extra inputs are not permitted; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].path_pdf: Extra inputs are not permitted; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].path_png: Extra inputs are not permitted; figures.2.function-after[validate_panel_lengths(), RichFigureManifestFigure].series_per_panel: Extra inputs are not permitted; Missing required figure from strategy: overview_schematic; Missing required figure from strategy: final_efficiency_comparison; paper.tex references figure missing from figure_manifest.json: figures/overview_schematic.png; paper.tex references figure missing from figure_manifest.json: figures/final_efficiency_comparison.pdf; paper.tex references figure missing from figure_manifest.json: figures/search_variant_comparison.pdf\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5822`\n- substantive_element_count: `7`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `46`\n- resolved_references: `21`\n- anchored_references: `25`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `5`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `1`\n- summary: Headline held-out and development numbers, qualification facts and planner implementation details trace to the evidence, but one control-efficiency interpretation overreads an aggregate mean.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `controls-matched-overread-1` (Results / Controls and diagnostics): Control efficiency is a mean of capped per-episode cost ratios averaged over boards and conditions, so 1.0002 does not show that plan costs matched their references. Offsetting savings and losses could produce the same mean, which the paper itself says for main efficiency in \"Reading an efficiency near 1\". The development controls average 0.9967 for all three configurations, so control plans on those data were not simply matched to their references. The final control efficiency is a single aggregate with no per-case or per-condition breakdown.\n  - claim: The retained control efficiency of 1.0002 indicates that, on permuted instances and on shuffled-null matrices, verified plan costs matched their own references on average to within about 2e-4.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Section Controls and diagnostics, and paragraph \"Reading an efficiency near 1\" saying the decomposition is unknown; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency=1.0001937677875696 with no per-case control records; source_evidence/evaluate_final.py header defining control_efficiency as a mean over boards of the mean of the permuted and shuffled-null efficiencies; source_evidence/search_metrics/config_0*_metrics.json control_efficiency=0.99671 to 0.99672; claims_grounding.json claim[4] supports only the value 1.0002.\n  - requested_fix: Reword to say only that mean control efficiency is 1.0002, so on average the planner's control-case efficiency relative to the evaluator's references was close to 1. Add that the aggregate cannot show per-case cost agreement, because savings and losses may offset, and that the development controls averaged 0.9967.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is mostly well-supported. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. Three targeted retrieval needs remain.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Priced, swap and weighted bribery under Copeland, Borda and plurality.\n- Open LLM Leaderboard (v2) documentation.\n- Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML evaluation.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. `related-work-social-choice-bribery` (related work, high, comparator)\n- **Anchor:** \"Our problem is a continuous variant: the 'voters' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores...\"\n- **Why:** The paper positions itself against manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems, but the cited works are generic or about election attacks. It cites nothing on cost-priced or swap bribery, or on bribery and control under Copeland, Borda and plurality. Those are the closest comparators for the rules the planner handles heuristically.\n- **Search topics:** priced and swap bribery; Copeland, Borda and plurality bribery and control; bribery under score-based voting with per-voter costs.\n\n### 2. `heldout-data-open-llm-leaderboard` (related work, high, benchmark_context)\n- **Anchor:** \"The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks\"\n- **Why:** The whole held-out evaluation uses Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots. The draft cites only the component benchmarks, not the leaderboard itself.\n- **Search topics:** Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; evaluation-harness documentation.\n\n### 3. `intro-incentive-gaming-claim` (introduction, medium, background)\n- **Anchor:** \"Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them\"\n- **Why:** This is the premise of the motivating question and it has no support. One or two Goodhart or leaderboard-incentive references would be enough.\n- **Search topics:** Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML benchmarks; benchmark culture and leaderboard incentives.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Software citations (NumPy, SciPy, MILP solver). These are low value for the narrative.\n- Planner heuristics such as bisection and greedy search. They are standard and described in the paper itself.\n- The contamination and leaderboard-gaming paragraph, which is already densely cited.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
