nner cost-efficiency under the stated investment model, not a general or causal claim about real developer training or about which aggregation rule is most robust.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain a completed, qualified final evaluation (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true) with five metrics and two final seeds, plus a three-configuration search on HELM MMLU. They do NOT contain per-rule or per-target cost tables, so the original open question (how required investment varies with aggregation rule) cannot be answered with numbers from the artifacts. The strongest honest story is therefore a method-and-evaluation paper: formalize targeted investment (L1 point budget and L0 task count, caps, field, targets, success = strictly first), define the rule set, describe the verified planner and trusted verifier/reference/baselines protocol, and report efficiency relative to the reference with controls, while stating explicitly that rule-dependence of minimal cost is a hypothesis in the task contract and is not supported or refuted by retained metrics. Efficiency values slightly above 1 mean verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference solver's (efficiency is capped at 2 per episode), so the reference is not claimed optimal and the planner is not claimed optimal either.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper directory or edit context exists. The framing is a fresh draft; the task brief's open-ended 'how does required investment vary by rule' question is retained as motivation, but the headline claim is narrowed to what the final evaluation measured.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or edit context was present; framing derived only from paper_data.json, task/task_contract.json, and the evaluator docstring.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study block; there is no frozen-selection vs baseline search-score comparison beyond the three search attempts, so no downstream-study claims are made.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verified minimal-investment planner (selected configuration: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality, verified heuristic or MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score, plus verified boundary polishing and fallback)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the studied method: the frozen search winner whose sealed final evaluation is the central result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive investment baselines (uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost; per case the better of the two)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The only baseline with a retained final metric (baseline_main_efficiency = 0.8166); needed for the comparison that qualified the run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference solver\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Defines the efficiency scale (episode efficiency = min(2, reference cost / verified plan cost)); readers cannot interpret 1.003 without it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Control conditions (permuted-input and within-task shuffled-score null)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"control_efficiency (final 1.0002; search 0.9967) is the retained diagnostic that the planner is not exploiting input order; report with the caveat that the retained value is an aggregate and that candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage configuration variants (basic solvers, +MILP level selection, +boundary polishing) on HELM MMLU v1.13.0\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Three scored attempts (0 unscored) give a small incremental-component comparison: main efficiency 1.0031, 1.0031, 1.0051; the MILP layer yields identical combined score to the basic version, boundary polishing yields +0.0016 combined. Keep as an ablation, never as the main result, and keep separate from final results.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final (sealed, Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001 and 9002): main efficiency 1.0032 vs naive-baseline 0.8166; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0; qualified. Source ids: final.metric.main_efficiency, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency, final.metric.control_efficiency, final.metric.plan_validity_rate, final.metric.combined_score, final.qualification.*, final.evidence.episodes.\",\n    \"Per-seed final episode scores (combined 1.00260 and 1.00257): the only variability evidence; report both values, state n=2 and that no confidence interval or significance test is available from the artifacts.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison on HELM MMLU (3 scored attempts, none unscored): main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051, control 0.9967 / 0.9967 / 0.9967, validity 1.0; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339. Report the +0.0016 gain from boundary polishing as small and unreplicated beyond 2 search seeds; the MILP layer showed no measured difference.\",\n    \"Search-to-final transfer: the selected configuration's main efficiency was 1.0051 on HELM search and 1.0032 on final boards; present as a descriptive observation, not as proof of generalization (different data, different boards).\",\n    \"Explicit scope statement: retained metrics contain no per-rule costs, so the claim that minimal required investment varies with aggregation rule is NOT evidenced here; list it as an untested hypothesis from the task contract and as the main limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Overview schematic (page 1-2): leaderboard score matrix -> investment model (per-task gains, L1 point budget with cap, L0 task count with cap at field-best) -> aggregation rules -> planner -> trusted verifier -> efficiency relative to reference; shows target strictly first as success.\",\n    \"Final-evaluation comparison: grouped bars for planner vs naive baselines vs reference line (efficiency=1) on the final boards, with per-seed markers.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison: grouped bars of main and control efficiency for the three configuration variants on HELM MMLU, with y-axis zoomed and labeled to show that differences are in the third decimal.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the problem: investment model, aggregation rules, planner/verifier pipeline, and how efficiency is defined relative to the reference solver.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column with text width about 5.5 in; a left-to-right pipeline with 5-6 labeled boxes needs the full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels. Place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_efficiency_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show final main efficiency of the planner against the naive-baseline score and the reference level of 1.0, plus control efficiency and plan validity, with per-seed values.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"efficiency relative to reference solver (main, control)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n{\n  \"agentic_evidence_audit\": {\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"finding_count\": 1,\n    \"findings\": [\n      {\n        \"claim\": \"The retained control efficiency of 1.0002 indicates that, on permuted instances and on shuffled-null matrices, verified plan costs matched their own references on average to within about 2e-4.\",\n        \"evidence\": \"paper.tex Section Controls and diagnostics, and paragraph \\\"Reading an efficiency near 1\\\" saying the decomposition is unknown; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency=1.0001937677875696 with no per-case control records; source_evidence/evalua44:This paper takes a first, deliberately narrow step: it makes the question computable and checks that the computation can be done reliably. We (i) define a declared investment model and two cost measures, (ii) specify eight aggregation rules and the strict-first success criterion, and (iii) study a planner whose every output is re-verified against an exact replica of the rule before it is returned. The planner is assessed on held-out public leaderboard snapshots by an evaluator that owns the rule engine, the plan verifier, a reference solver, naive baselines and control conditions; candidate-reported diagnostics are never trusted.\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations on HELM MMLU (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. The closest discrete analogues price the intervention: bribery models give each voter a price for changing their vote \\citep{Faliszewski2006HowHI}, and swap bribery charges for each exchange of adjacent candidates in a voter's ranking \\citep{Elkind2009SwapB}. Complexity results depend on the rule and on tie-breaking; for example, Copeland voting has been studied for its resistance to bribery and control \\citep{Faliszewski2009LlullAC}, and tie-breaking conventions change manipulation complexity \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC}. We cite these results as context only: we do not prove hardness for the planner's problems and do not claim that the planner's heuristics inherit any of these guarantees. Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores (not preferences directly), and cost is measured in score points or in touched tasks. We borrow the strict-win convention, under which ties count against the target.\n73:A leaderboard is a matrix $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ of scores of $N$ models on $T$ tasks (higher is better). An aggregation rule $r$ maps $X$ to a vector $v^r(X)\\in\\mathbb{R}^N$. A developer controls a target row $i$ and can choose gains $g\\in\\mathbb{R}^T_{\\ge 0}$, replacing $x_i$ by $x_i+g$ while leaving every other row unchanged; there is no spillover across tasks and no effect on other models. Gains are bounded by per-task caps $c_t$, so $g_t\\le c_t-x_{it}$. Two cost measures are used:\n132:The evaluator owns the rule engine, the plan verifier, the reference solver, the naive baselines and the controls. The \\emph{episode efficiency} of a verified successful plan is $\\min\\{2,\\,\\max(\\mathrm{ref},\\epsilon)/\\max(\\mathrm{cost},\\epsilon)\\}$, where $\\mathrm{ref}$ is the reference solver's cost and costs below 0.01 points count as 0.01 (L0 costs are counts). A correct infeasibility claim, or a correct empty plan for an already-first target, scores 1; any other outcome, including malformed or timed-out responses, scores 0 and is never retried or dropped. The reference solver is exact for mean, maximin and odd-$T$ median and search-based otherwise, and its candidate set includes the polished naive baselines for L1; hence an average efficiency above 1 means the planner's verified plans were on average slightly cheaper than the reference plans, not that the reference is optimal.\n134:\\emph{Main efficiency} averages over boards, over main cases (8 rules $\\times$ 2 targets $\\times$ 2 cost measures per board) and over evaluator seeds. \\emph{Control efficiency} averages two conditions per board and rule: a permuted-input control (non-identity row and task permutations, reference cost invariant) and a within-task shuffled-score null (columns shuffled across models, target set to the rank-2 model of the shuffled matrix, own reference). \\emph{Plan validity rate} is the fraction of declared episodes with a well-formed in-bounds response. The \\emph{combined} score is $0.8\\times$main $+\\,0.2\\times$control. The naive baselines (uniform gain and greedy marginal-cost) are scored against the same reference, taking the better of the two per case. A held-out evaluation is \\emph{qualified} when integrity checks pass, main efficiency strictly exceeds the baseline's, and (when time permits) a replayed first episode reproduces its verified cost.\n146:\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the reference level of 1. (b) Main, control and combined efficiency of the planner on a zoomed axis; dots are the two per-seed combined values.}\n153:so no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\n158:Three observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n160:\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n162:\\subsection{Controls and diagnostics}\n163:The retained control efficiency of 1.0002 indicates that, on permuted instances and on shuffled-null matrices, verified plan costs matched their own references on average to within about $2\\times10^{-4}$. These controls check invariance to row and task order and non-vacuity of the instance; they do not probe whether the planner overfits the Open LLM boards. The evaluator recomputes every plan's validity and cost itself; candidate-reported diagnostics are marked unverified in the retained evidence and were not used.\n176:The two seeds of each configuration agree to within $3\\times10^{-6}$ in combined score, which shows reproducibility of the evaluator's computation for a fixed matrix but is not a test of the configuration difference. Control efficiency is 0.9967 for Base and Base+MILP and 0.9967 for Base+Polish, % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/config_03_metrics.json#control_efficiency]]\n236:\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules}, and L1 or L0 costs. Per-task headroom is $h_t=c_t-x_{it}$. Rule definitions follow the evaluator's rule engine: Borda scores are minus the mean per-task rank (ties share half credit), plurality counts tasks on which a model is the unique maximizer, Copeland counts pairwise task-majority wins with half credit for ties, and the trimmed mean drops $\\lfloor T/5\\rfloor$ tasks from each end. The main cases are the 32 combinations of rule, target rank (2, 10) and cost measure per board; control cases use rank 2 and L1 for each of the 8 rules, once for the permuted input and once for the shuffled null.\n257:Control efficiency & 1.0001938 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\n259:Combined score ($0.8\\,$main$+0.2\\,$control) & 1.0025871 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nbuild_figures.py","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff70e5e4-c0ea-42eb-940e-6ce62303ebf1","uuid":"2a36b0d4-3b7d-47d0-99a1-f6b2a6f5f82d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:29:19.096Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/overview_schematic.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/overview_schematic.prompts.json\",\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_efficiency_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 8,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 8.5\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/final_efficiency_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/final_efficiency_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.main_efficiency\",\n        \"final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency\",\n        \"final.metric.control_efficiency\",\n        \"final.metric.combined_score\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"search_variant_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 8,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 8.5\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/search_variant_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/search_variant_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.top_program_01\",\n        \"result.top_program_02\",\n        \"result.top_program_03\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.pdf\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.png\nfinal_efficiency_comparison.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.png\noverview_schematic.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.prompts.json\nsearch_variant_comparison.pdf\nsearch_variant_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nsearch_variant_comparison.png\nsearch_variant_comparison.png.prov.json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\",\n  \"central_question\": \"Under a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive investment baselines?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the sealed final evaluation over Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, a verifier-checked investment planner (selected on HELM MMLU v1.13.0) reached a qualified outcome: its mean efficiency relative to the reference solver was 1.0032, versus 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every plan well-formed; the evidence supports a narrow, conditional claim about planner cost-efficiency under the stated investment model, not a general or causal claim about real developer training or about which aggregation rule is most robust.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain a completed, qualified final evaluation (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true) with five metrics and two final seeds, plus a three-configuration search on HELM MMLU. They do NOT contain per-rule or per-target cost tables, so the original open question (how required investment varies with aggregation rule) cannot be answered with numbers from the artifacts. The strongest honest story is therefore a method-and-evaluation paper: formalize targeted investment (L1 point budget and L0 task count, caps, field, targets, success = strictly first), define the rule set, describe the verified planner and trusted verifier/reference/baselines protocol, and report efficiency relative to the reference with controls, while stating explicitly that rule-dependence of minimal cost is a hypothesis in the task contract and is not supported or refuted by retained metrics. Efficiency values slightly above 1 mean verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference solver's (efficiency is capped at 2 per episode), so the reference is not claimed optimal and the planner is not claimed optimal either.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper directory or edit context exists. The framing is a fresh draft; the task brief's open-ended 'how does required investment vary by rule' question is retained as motivation, but the headline claim is narrowed to what the final evaluation measured.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or edit context was present; framing derived only from paper_data.json, task/task_contract.json, and the evaluator docstring.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study block; there is no frozen-selection vs baseline search-score comparison beyond the three search attempts, so no downstream-study claims are made.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verified minimal-investment planner (selected configuration: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality, verified heuristic or MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score, plus verified boundary polishing and fallback)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the studied method: the frozen search winner whose sealed final evaluation is the central result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive investment baselines (uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost; per case the better of the two)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The only baseline with a retained final metric (baseline_main_efficiency = 0.8166); needed for the comparison that qualified the run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference solver\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Defines the efficiency scale (episode efficiency = min(2, reference cost / verified plan cost)); readers cannot interpret 1.003 without it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Control conditions (permuted-input and within-task shuffled-score null)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"control_efficiency (final 1.0002; search 0.9967) is the retained diagnostic that the planner is not exploiting input order; report with the caveat that the retained value is an aggregate and that candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage configuration variants (basic solvers, +MILP level selection, +boundary polishing) on HELM MMLU v1.13.0\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Three scored attempts (0 unscored) give a small incremental-component comparison: main efficiency 1.0031, 1.0031, 1.0051; the MILP layer yields identical combined score to the basic version, boundary polishing yields +0.0016 combined. Keep as an ablation, never as the main result, and keep separate from final results.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final (sealed, Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001 and 9002): main efficiency 1.0032 vs naive-baseline 0.8166; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0; qualified. Source ids: final.metric.main_efficiency, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency, final.metric.control_efficiency, final.metric.plan_validity_rate, final.metric.combined_score, final.qualification.*, final.evidence.episodes.\",\n    \"Per-seed final episode scores (combined 1.00260 and 1.00257): the only variability evidence; report both values, state n=2 and that no confidence interval or significance test is available from the artifacts.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison on HELM MMLU (3 scored attempts, none unscored): main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051, control 0.9967 / 0.9967 / 0.9967, validity 1.0; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339. Report the +0.0016 gain from boundary polishing as small and unreplicated beyond 2 search seeds; the MILP layer showed no measured difference.\",\n    \"Search-to-final transfer: the selected configuration's main efficiency was 1.0051 on HELM search and 1.0032 on final boards; present as a descriptive observation, not as proof of generalization (different data, different boards).\",\n    \"Explicit scope statement: retained metrics contain no per-rule costs, so the claim that minimal required investment varies with aggregation rule is NOT evidenced here; list it as an untested hypothesis from the task contract and as the main limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Overview schematic (page 1-2): leaderboard score matrix -> investment model (per-task gains, L1 point budget with cap, L0 task count with cap at field-best) -> aggregation rules -> planner -> trusted verifier -> efficiency relative to reference; shows target strictly first as success.\",\n    \"Final-evaluation comparison: grouped bars for planner vs naive baselines vs reference line (efficiency=1) on the final boards, with per-seed markers.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison: grouped bars of main and control efficiency for the three configuration variants on HELM MMLU, with y-axis zoomed and labeled to show that differences are in the third decimal.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the problem: investment model, aggregation rules, planner/verifier pipeline, and how efficiency is defined relative to the reference solver.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column with text width about 5.5 in; a left-to-right pipeline with 5-6 labeled boxes needs the full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels. Place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_efficiency_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show final main efficiency of the planner against the naive-baseline score and the reference level of 1.0, plus control efficiency and plan validity, with per-seed values.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"efficiency relative to reference solver (main, control)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n{\n  \"agentic_evidence_audit\": {\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"finding_count\": 1,\n    \"findings\": [\n      {\n        \"claim\": \"The retained control efficiency of 1.0002 indicates that, on permuted instances and on shuffled-null matrices, verified plan costs matched their own references on average to within about 2e-4.\",\n        \"evidence\": \"paper.tex Section Controls and diagnostics, and paragraph \\\"Reading an efficiency near 1\\\" saying the decomposition is unknown; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency=1.0001937677875696 with no per-case control records; source_evidence/evalua44:This paper takes a first, deliberately narrow step: it makes the question computable and checks that the computation can be done reliably. We (i) define a declared investment model and two cost measures, (ii) specify eight aggregation rules and the strict-first success cr