{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017LCcBFDhFDZsY2nwxjaAN2","type":"tool_result","content":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\nThe held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\nversus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\na difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\nso no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\nand the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nPer-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nand each seed individually satisfies the gate that main efficiency exceeds the baseline's.\n\nThree observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n\n\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and diagnostics}\nThe retained control efficiency of 1.0002 indicates that, on permuted instances and on shuffled-null matrices, verified plan costs matched their own references on average to within about $2\\times10^{-4}$. These controls check invariance to row and task order and non-vacuity of the instance; they do not probe whether the planner overfits the Open LLM boards. The evaluator recomputes every plan's validity and cost itself; candidate-reported diagnostics are marked unverified in the retained evidence and were not used.\n\n\\subsection{Component comparison on the development leaderboard}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n35:   \"claim\": \"Control efficiency is 1.0002 and the combined score is 1.0026 (0.8 main + 0.2 control).\",\n37:    \"final.metric.control_efficiency\",\n68:   \"claim\": \"Development main efficiencies are 1.0031, 1.0031 and 1.0051 and control efficiency 0.9967 for all three, read from the retained search metric files.\",\n44:This paper takes a first, deliberately narrow step: it makes the question computable and checks that the computation can be done reliably. We (i) define a declared investment model and two cost mea\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations o\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,\n73:A leaderboard is a matrix $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ of scores of $N$ models on $T$ tasks (higher is better). An aggregation rule $r$ maps $X$ to a vector $v^r(X)\\in\\mathbb{R}^N$. A developer contro\n176:The two seeds of each configuration agree to within $3\\times10^{-6}$ in combined score, which shows reproducibility of the evaluator's computation for a fixed matrix but is not a test of the confi\n236:\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules},\n257:Control efficiency & 1.0001938 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\n259:Combined score ($0.8\\,$main$+0.2\\,$control) & 1.0025871 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff70e5e4-c0ea-42eb-940e-6ce62303ebf1","uuid":"824bcad0-5bb0-438d-9a66-e236015be6c2","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:29:22.678Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\nThe held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\nversus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\na difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\nso no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\nand the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nPer-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nand each seed individually satisfies the gate that main efficiency exceeds the baseline's.\n\nThree observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n\n\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and diagnostics}\nThe retained 